<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Please evaluate and advise on type 2 diabetes mellitus with diabetic autonomic (poly)neuropathy and orthostatic hypotension and type 2 diabetes mellitus with diabetic polyneuropathy and primary open-angle glaucoma, bilateral, severe stage and type 2 diabetes mellitus with mild nonproliferative diabetic retinopathy without macular edema, bilateral and hyperlipidemia, unspecified and personal history of pneumonia (recurrent). [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Endocrinology consultation to confirm diabetic autonomic neuropathy as the cause of orthostatic hypotension, reconsider the earlier Shy-Drager label, and choose add-on glucose-lowering therapy. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Seen in endocrinology consultation at the request of Gideon Hale, MD. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Lightheaded when he stands and afraid of another fall; was once told he had Shy-Drager syndrome and wants to know what he really has. [S]</item><item>Wants to keep the sight he has left so he can keep writing songs. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>J. R. (Johnny) Cash was born on February 26, 1932, in Kingsland, Cleveland County, Arkansas, to Ray and Carrie Cash. [D]</item><item>Cash&apos;s older brother Jack died in a sawmill accident in 1944. [D]</item><item>In 1957 Cash began taking amphetamines to stay awake on a long road trip, the start of an addiction that lasted about a decade; in a 2002 interview he named amphetamines and barbiturates in the 1960s. [D]</item><item>Cash was hospitalized in 1983 with internal bleeding; after recovering he entered the Betty Ford Clinic for treatment. [D]</item><item>In October 1997 Cash was diagnosed with Shy-Drager syndrome, an illness similar to Parkinson&apos;s disease; in a 2000 interview he said the diagnosis was a misdiagnosis. [D]</item><item>Cash later learned he had autonomic neuropathy; in November 2002 he described it as a deadening of the nerve endings in his lower extremities that had taken the strength from his legs, so that he could walk, but not very well. [D]</item><item>Cash was repeatedly hospitalized for pneumonia in his last years; he was treated for pneumonia at Baptist Hospital in Nashville in February 2001, and in November 2002 he said he had had pneumonia four times in three years. [D]</item><item>In November 2002 Cash said about 60 percent of his vision was gone, which he attributed to the neuropathy and the diabetes, and he said he hoped the glaucoma could also be reversed. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

